package {{ package }}
{# `round(x)` lowers to `math.Round`. Go makes an unused import a COMPILE
   ERROR, so this cannot be added unconditionally the way a C++ include could
   — every transform that does not round would stop building. The condition
   reads the emitted bodies for the same reason as the C++ side: a flag
   computed upstream could disagree with what the emitter produced, and here
   that disagreement is a broken build in both directions. #}
{# ⚠ `math.` rather than `math.Round`: `floor(x)` lowers to `math.Floor`, and a
   condition naming ONE function goes stale the moment the vocabulary grows —
   which it did. Any `math.` in an emitted body is the math package and nothing
   else, so this form cannot fall behind `real_to_int_builtin`. #}
